# Discussion & Conclusions

The team worked through the scenario from detection to full recovery and the all-clear, covering both system recovery (DR) and continuity of operations and communications (BC). The procedures in the Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery Plan were broadly validated as accurate, and the team was able to recover within the documented targets. A small number of improvement areas were identified.

## What worked
- Detection and coordination over Slack were clear; the incident-lead role and escalation path were understood.
- The restore-from-backup path was understood end to end — restore the latest automated Azure backup to a fresh instance, verify data integrity, and cut back over.
- RTO/RPO targets (Appendix B) were reviewed and judged realistic: a ~3–4h restore sits inside the **12h RTO**, and ~12h of data loss sits inside the **24h RPO**.
- Customer communication ownership (CEO + Customer Support) was clear.

## Findings / areas to improve
- **The process and BC/DR plan can be simplified.** We understand why the plan and its process exist and want to keep that intent, but in a live incident some steps add overhead. The plan should be trimmed — and a lightweight "first response" quick-card added — so that bureaucracy never stands between us and fixing the issue as fast as possible (e.g., engineering begins recovery immediately while formal roles and comms spin up in parallel).
- **No ready-made customer outage-comms template.** Drafting a status message under pressure costs time; a pre-approved template would speed external comms.
- **Post-restore data reconciliation is undefined.** Restoring loses the transactions between the last backup and the incident (~12h here); there is no documented step to reconcile or replay them.
- **The restore has not been recently drilled hands-on.** The procedure is understood in theory; a periodic real restore test would raise confidence (and satisfies the plan's annual backup-restoration requirement).

## Conclusions
- The BC/DR plan is fundamentally sound and the team can recover the product within target.
- Highest-value improvement is to **simplify the response process for speed**, pre-stage customer comms, and drill the restore.

